You may, or may not know, that it is possible for languages to include a styles.php file, and then if the current theme specifies both $THEME->standardsheets = true and $THEME->langsheets = true (both of those are the default), then the styles.php file from the language folder will be included with the rest of the standard theme CSS . Well, that was the situation in Moodle 1.9. However, looking in CVS , only Vietnamese includes a styles.php file (last modified in 2005), and that only says 
```text
body,td,tr,li {
 font-family: Arial, Verdana, Helvetica, sans-serif !important;
}
```
 Which looks very similar to the CSS in the standard theme: 
```text
body, table, td, th, li {
 font-family:Arial, Verdana, Helvetica, sans-serif;
}
```
 I can't work out why this was introduced. ( http://cvs.moodle.org/moodle/theme/standard/config.php?r1=1.13&r2=1.14 does not help much.) The one thing that might need it is right-to-left languages. However, these days we have a better way to handle that, using an optional rtl.css file in the theme folder. So, I am proposing to remove this functionality from Moodle 2.0. If anyone objects to that, please explain why ASAP. Thanks.

Hi all. There were several discussions about how to handle plurals and other grammar specific issues when translating Moodle strings. See for example: http://moodle.org/mod/forum/discuss.php?d=27431 http://moodle.org/mod/forum/discuss.php?d=40022 http://tracker.moodle.org/browse/MDL-4790 I have implemented a quick hack and would like to hear your comments about this approach. I have read about gettext library support in PHP. But finally I have decided to use Smarty syntax. It allows translator to use programming-like constructs when dealing with language grammar. Let us have for example this string (see message.php) 
```text
$string['readmessages'] = '$a read messages'; // in English
```
 The Czech language as well as many others (like Slovenian) uses different forms of the word “messages” according to their number, i.e. $a. So far, we need to translate these strings in a way like: 
```text
$string['readmessages'] = 'The number of read messages: $a'; // in English
$string['readmessages'] = 'Počet přečtených zpráv: $a'; // in Czech
```
 but this does not suite our needs very well and it is not acceptable at all in some languages. So, my hack allows translators to use following Smarty syntax: 
```text
$string['readmessages'] = '{if $a eq 1} 1 přečtená zpráva {elseif ($a>=2 && $a<=4)} $a přečtené zprávy {else} $a přečtených zpráv{/if}';
```
 This syntax may be used in other scenarios as well. Imagine: 
```text
$string['updatethis'] = '{if \"$a\" eq \"Forum\"}Edit this Forum{elseif \"$a\" eq \"Quiz\"}Edit this Quiz ... // continues
```
 (this may not make sense in English but it may be important e.g. to use different forms of the word “this” in other languages etc.) I have written a quick wrapper around the current get_string() function. To try it, proceed following steps: create new file in $CFG-> dirroot /lang (where only en/ and en_utf8/ directories should live these days) named default_string.tpl with this content: 
```text
{eval var=$tpl}
```
 modify lib/moodlelib.php file and rename the current get_string() function to _get_ string() add following wrapper: 
```text
/**
 * The smarty wrapper around the original get_string.
 */
function get_string($identifier, $module='', $a=NULL, $extralocations=NULL) {

 global $CFG;

 //
 // Set up smarty template system.
 // This is here just because of a quick hack. this should be moved somewhere
 // into the lib/setup.php and should be replaced by
 // global $STRING_PARSER;
 //
 require_once($CFG->libdir .'/smarty/Smarty.class.php');
 $STRING_PARSER = new Smarty;
 $STRING_PARSER->template_dir = $CFG->dirroot .'/lang';
 if (!file_exists($CFG->
dataroot
 .'/cache/smarty')) {
 make_upload_directory('cache/smarty');
 }
 $STRING_PARSER->compile_dir = $CFG->dataroot .'/cache/smarty';

 //
 // Let us get the localised string in the old good way
 //
 $tpl = _get_string($identifier,$module, $a, $extralocations);

 //
 // Now we could decide whether to parse this string with Smarty.
 // E.g. if we are using '$string[identifier] = 'smarty:Here comes localised string';
 // we could do simple if substring(1,7)=='smarty:' etc.
 // I will parse all string here for simplicity (test performance please).
 //
 // The localized string forms a template to be parsed.
 //
 $STRING_PARSER->assign('tpl', $tpl);
 return $STRING_PARSER->fetch('default_string.tpl');
}
```
 If we agree on merging this approach into HEAD, we will need to do more testing (e.g. check syntax when saving lang files, improve the hack performance etc.). Also, I do not know what plans are with Smarty library in Moodle distribution so, please, take this just as a proposal Thank you for any comments!

